{"event": "LIFE_CYCLE_COMPLETED", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T09:37:17.930640+00:00", "cycle": 12, "goal_id": "federation", "padge_response": "{\"goal\": \"Add endpoint connectivity checker utility for federation components\", \"reason\": \"Federation workflows need a deterministic way to verify that participant endpoint URLs are reachable (DNS resolves and TCP port is open) without relying on external higher‑level services. Implementing a lightweight, offline‑compatible connectivity checker enables controlled candidate validation and improves safety before proposal assimilation.\", \"changes\": [{\"path\": \"omega/federation_endpoint_connectivity_checker.py\", \"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"omega.federation_endpoint_connectivity_checker\\n==============================================\\nUtility module that provides a deterministic, offline‑compatible check of the\\nnetwork connectivity for federation participant endpoints.\\n\\nThe function :func:`check_connectivity` parses a URL, resolves the hostname\\n(using the system resolver) and attempts a TCP connection to the appropriate\\nport with a configurable timeout. The implementation avoids any external\\nservices – it merely uses the Python standard library (`socket` and\\n`urllib.parse`).\\n\\nThe checker is deliberately lightweight:\\n* It does **not** perform TLS hand‑shakes; it only verifies that a TCP socket\\n  can be opened.\\n* It respects a short default timeout (5 seconds) to keep tests fast and\\n  deterministic.\\n* All errors are caught and translated into a simple ``bool`` result – ``True``\\n  indicates that the endpoint is reachable, ``False`` otherwise.\\n\\nUsage example\\n-------------\\n```python\\nfrom omega.federation_endpoint_connectivity_checker import check_connectivity\\n\\nurl = \\\"https://node.example.org/api\\\"\\nif check_connectivity(url):\\n    print(\\\"Endpoint reachable\\\")\\nelse:\\n    print(\\\"Endpoint not reachable\\\")\\n```\\n\\nThis utility can be composed with the existing validator\\n(`omega.federation_endpoint_validator`) and candidate processor to build a\\nfull discovery‑validation pipeline that includes connectivity guarantees.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nfrom __future__ import annotations\\n\\nimport socket\\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\\nfrom typing import Tuple\\n\\n__all__: list[str] = [\\\"check_connectivity\\\"]\\n\\n\\ndef _default_port(scheme: str) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return the conventional port for a given URL scheme.\\n\\n    Parameters\\n    ----------\\n    scheme: str\\n        The URL scheme (e.g., ``\\\"https\\\"`` or ``\\\"http\\\"``).\\n\\n    Returns\\n    -------\\n    int\\n        The default port number for the scheme.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if scheme == \\\"https\\\":\\n        return 443\\n    if scheme == \\\"http\\\":\\n        return 80\\n    # Fallback to 443 for unknown secure‑like schemes, otherwise 80.\\n    return 443 if scheme.endswith(\\\"s\\\") else 80\\n\\n\\ndef _extract_host_port(url: str) -> Tuple[str, int]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Parse *url* and return a ``(hostname, port)`` tuple.\\n\\n    The function validates that the scheme is HTTP or HTTPS and resolves the\\n    port either from the URL itself or from the default for the scheme.\\n    It raises ``ValueError`` for malformed inputs.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    parsed = urlparse(url)\\n    if parsed.scheme not in {\\\"http\\\", \\\"https\\\"}:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Unsupported URL scheme '{parsed.scheme}'. Only http/https are allowed.\\\")\\n    hostname = parsed.hostname\\n    if not hostname:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"URL must contain a hostname.\\\")\\n    port = parsed.port if parsed.port is not None else _default_port(parsed.scheme)\\n    return hostname, port\\n\\n\\ndef check_connectivity(url: str, timeout: float = 5.0) -> bool:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Check TCP connectivity to the host defined by *url*.\\n\\n    The function performs a synchronous socket connection attempt to the host\\n    and port derived from *url*. It returns ``True`` if the connection can be\\n    established within *timeout* seconds, otherwise ``False``.\\n\\n    Parameters\\n    ----------\\n    url: str\\n        The endpoint URL to test (must be ``http`` or ``https``).\\n    timeout: float, optional\\n        The maximum time in seconds to wait for the connection.\\n\\n    Returns\\n    -------\\n    bool\\n        ``True`` if the endpoint is reachable, ``False`` otherwise.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    try:\\n        host, port = _extract_host_port(url)\\n        # ``socket.create_connection`` performs DNS resolution and attempts the TCP connect.\\n        with socket.create_connection((host, port), timeout=timeout):\\n            return True\\n    except (OSError, ValueError):\\n        # OSError covers DNS failures, connection refusals, timeouts, etc.\\n        return False\\n\"}]}", "execution": "PADGE_RESPONSE_RECEIVED", "code_integration": "NOT_ASSERTED"}
